4. Solution design
4.1 The agents
Each agent does one job. Small models are enough for most specialists; use a mid-size model where reasoning and writing quality matter.
Agent	Its one job	MCP tools it may use	Model
Supervisor (LangGraph node)	Reads case status, picks the next specialist using structured output and merges results into graph state. Code guardrails reject out-of-order moves.	case-mcp: get_case, case:// resource	Mid-size
Intake agent	Registers the claim, checks policy coverage and flags likely duplicates.	policy-mcp: get_policy, check_coverage; case-mcp: find_similar_cases, register_case	Small
Document agent	Runs OCR, classification and field extraction on each document in parallel, validates them and flags low confidence.	documents-mcp: list_documents, extract_text, classify_document, extract_fields, validate_case_documents	Small
Summary agent	Writes the case summary (facts, evidence, missing info, risks, next action) and cites the policy clause behind each risk.	case-mcp: get_case, save_summary; documents-mcp: list_documents; policy-mcp: search_policy_wording; prompt case_summary	Mid-size
Routing agent	Picks an adjuster and an approver whose approval limit covers the claim, then requests approval. It never approves.	workflow-mcp: get_workload, assign_case, request_approval	Small
SLA & comms agent	Sets SLA timers, emails the approver and claimant, requests missing documents and sends decision letters.	workflow-mcp: schedule_sla, send_notification, request_documents; prompts claimant_letter, approver_brief	Small
4.2 The MCP servers
Remember the three MCP building blocks: tools are actions the model can call, resources are read-only context addressed by a URI, and prompts are reusable templates.
Server	Tools	Resources	Prompts
case-mcp	get_case, list_cases, find_similar_cases, register_case, save_summary	case://{case_id}, case://{case_id}/timeline	case_summary
policy-mcp	get_policy, check_coverage, search_policy_wording (pgvector search)	policy://{policy_number}, policy-wording://{product}	—
documents-mcp	list_documents, extract_text, classify_document, extract_fields, validate_case_documents	document://{case_id}/{doc_id}	—
workflow-mcp	get_workload, assign_case, request_approval, schedule_sla, send_notification, request_documents	staff://directory, sla://rules	claimant_letter, approver_brief

MCP good practices for this project:
•	Stateless servers — every call carries the case ID; all state lives in PostgreSQL.
•	Two transports — stdio while developing; Streamable HTTP between Docker containers.
•	Typed tools — use Python type hints and Pydantic return types so every tool has an input and output schema.
•	Friendly errors — return validation problems as a clear tool result, so the agent can correct its own call.
•	Test first — check every tool, resource and prompt in MCP Inspector before wiring up any agent.
•	Use current features — use the newest protocol version your SDK supports and avoid deprecated features such as Sampling, Roots and Logging.
4.3 The LangGraph orchestration
Concept	How you use it
State	ClaimState holds: case ID, messages, case status, next step, agent hops, stage results, human decision, errors and token usage.
Nodes	supervisor, the five specialists, human_review, await_documents, finalize and manual_review.
Routing	The supervisor returns Command(goto=...). A guard function allows only moves that fit the case status (for example, no routing before a summary exists). Repeated failures go to manual_review.
Parallel work	Send fans out one task per document; results are joined before validation.
Human-in-the-loop	interrupt() pauses the graph at human_review and await_documents. Streamlit resumes it with Command(resume=...) carrying the decision, rationale and approver.
Persistence	PostgresSaver checkpointer with thread ID = case ID, so a case can wait days and survive restarts. Checkpoint history allows replay for audits and debugging.
Streaming	stream_mode “updates” and “messages” show live agent progress in Streamlit.
Resilience	RetryPolicy on nodes, model fallback middleware and a recursion limit stop transient errors and endless loops.
